When p2phttp is serving many repositories, it currently needs to run
several git processes in each at startup. This can make startup take a
long time before it is listening to or responding to http requests.

It should be possible for p2phttp to start up quickly, without creating
an Annex state for each repository. And demand load it when a repositry is
accessed. (Or load them in the background as an idle time job.)

This would need a `uuid -> repository` map.

That could be a sqlite database that contains eg:

    UUID,InodeCache,OsPath

Where the InodeCache is of the git config file that was read to learn the
UUID. That way, in the unlikely event that a repository UUID changes, the
new UUID is learned on startup. (It is not safe to change UUID while 
git-annex is running of course.)

Another benefit of such a map is that, when a request comes in for an
unknown UUID, `p2phttp --directory` currently re-scans all repositories,
making Annex states for all of them. That blocks all other (new) requests
until complete. It should be possible to avoid any blocking of requests
for existing repos during that scan, and I think that should be done
to avoid any chance of DOS attack. But also using the map will speed up
the scan, and deman loading of Annex states will too. --[[Joey]]

[[!tag projects/INM7]]
